were done on 2026-09-24 and [21] (the --today glance) on 2026-09-25 (see their "DONE" notes below); the rest below are still open.  Line
references below are to trackview.py as of each item's date; flag names are
suggestions.  Ordered roughly by value; each notes the cheapest implementation hook.

Colouring, the map & the panels
[6] [done] Extend --color-by with {speed,power}.  The four telemetry metrics are
     already read in load_tracks (t["speed"]/t["power"], as [sum,count]); add them
     as shading modes (fast / high = hot, on a perceptually-uniform ramp) so a rider
     can read pacing and effort at a glance.  Per-segment colouring needs the per-
     point value, not just the [sum,count] average the tele dict keeps, so the values
     are retained as per-point arrays (the way grade and ele already are); absent
     --ext they fall back to flat.
     DONE (2026-09-20): `--color-by {speed,power}` joined the existing elev/grade/flat
     choices.  load_tracks now keeps per-decimated-point arrays d["speed_pts"] /
     d["power_pts"] (and d["tsec"]) by carrying a per-point record through
     decimate(carry=...), aligned with merc/ele/grade.  Each segment is shaded by the
     average of its two endpoint values on the perceptually-uniform CMAPEED="inferno"
     ramp (fast / high = hot); the norm is _metric_norm(), a robust [2 %, 98 %] range so
     one GPS spike can't squash the ramp (as the grade cap does for grade).  The
     colourbar reads "Speed (km/h)" / "Power (W)" and the title "shaded by speed/power".
     Telemetry is auto-collected (with_ext) when --color-by speed/power or --heat is set
     even without --ext (which still gates the avg-telemetry title line + ride-log
     columns); a ride with no per-point values falls back to its flat per-track colour
     and a note is printed.

[7] [done] A scale bar on every map.  render() already holds the Mercator bbox
     (w0,s0,e0,n0) and the chosen detail, so a labelled "0 .. N km" bar (a "nice"
     1/2/5 x 10^k length, drawn in a corner) is cheap.  A static map with no scale
     is the one thing a viewer can otherwise never read off the image.
     DONE (2026-09-19): _draw_scale_bar + _nice_m/_fmt_len put a "0 .. N km" bar in
     every map's lower-right corner (a nice 1/2/5 x 10^k of ~15 % of the width, in
     Web-Mercator metres; a white casing so it reads on a busy basemap).  --no-scale
     omits it; it is a map-only flag (ignored under --no-map).  Smoke-tested headlessly
     (no network) and confirmed on a real basemap render.

[8] [done] A density / "time-on-route" overlay.  Instead of one LineCollection per
     track, weight each segment by the time spent on it (or plot a 2-D histogram of
     the points) so a combined map of many rides shows *where you ride*, not just
     every route as one line - a --heat switch or a 5th --color-by mode.
     DONE (2026-09-20): the `--heat` switch (a map-only flag, ignored under --no-map)
     draws a 2-D "time on route" density *under* the route lines: a HEAT_GRID x
     HEAT_GRID (300x300) np.histogram2d over the bbox, each cell weighted by the time
     spent on the segments through it (so a slow climb / rest stop / traffic glows
     more than a fast pass), on a YlOrRd ramp at HEAT_ALPHA (0.55) opacity with empty
     cells masked so the basemap shows through, capped at HEAT_CAP_PCT (98 %).  Per-
     point dwell (seconds) comes from _dwell_times(d["tsec"], n) - point j carries the
     time on the segment leaving it, the last point its incoming one; a ride with no
     usable timestamps falls back to a uniform point-count weight (so --heat works
     without --ext too, and --heat auto-enables the timestamp collection).  It gets its
     own "Time on route" colourbar, ticks formatted as a duration by _fmt_heat_time
     (N s / N m / Nh Mm) via a FuncFormatter; when a metric colourbar (elev/grade/
     speed/power) already sits on the right edge the heat bar is suppressed with a note
     (one bar there).

[9] [done] Ride labels on a combined map.  A short name + distance near each start
     marker (a halo'd text label; _short_label already exists) makes routes
     identifiable without the colourbar, so a many-ride overview reads without a
     legend key.
     DONE (2026-09-20): the `--labels` switch (a map-only flag, ignored under --no-map)
     tags each route's start marker (on a combined map, len(tracks) > 1; a single
     ride's name is already in its title) with a halo'd "name      N km" line via
     ax.annotate(_short_label(name) + distance, offset, withStroke white halo).  Loop
     rides share one start, so the tag is staggered vertically by the track index
     (i % 12 * 3 pt) to keep a handful of co-located starts legible.

Analysis
[10] [done] Climb detection & ranking.  Scan each ride's t["grade"]/t["cum_km"] for
     sustained climbs (a min length + min average grade), score them a
     Strava/Komoot-style "climb score" (gain x a grade-weighted factor) and list
     the top N to stats/.  "Steepest / hardest climb" is the single most-wanted
     cycling stat and needs no telemetry.
     DONE (2026-09-19): _ride_climbs/_all_climbs/write_climbs + the --climbs [N] flag
      (default 10).  A "climb" is a maximal run of segments whose *smoothed* grade
       (_moving_avg, CLIMB_SMOOTH) stays >= CLIMB_MIN_GR (4 %) over >= CLIMB_MIN_LEN
       (250 m); ranked by a climb score = gain x average grade so a long moderate climb
     beats a short steep one.  Writes stats/trackview-<range>_climbs.txt (a self-
     describing header naming the thresholds).  Pure geometry, so it runs under --no-map.

[11] [done] More profile lines.  _draw_profile() today draws only elevation + grade.  Add
     speed / power / HR / cadence as further lines (a 3rd twin axis, or a slim
     stacked mini-panel per metric) from the same telemetry, so one figure is a full
     "ride card".  Needs the per-point arrays from item [6].
     DONE (2026-09-21): `--profile` (a single track, or --split) now renders a "ride card":
     the map, the elevation + grade silhouette, then one slim telemetry mini-panel per
     metric the ride carries, each on its own strip below and sharing the distance x-axis.
     The panels are data-driven from PROF_METRICS (a fixed order and colour -- power
     #d73027, speed #1f78b4, HR #2a9d8f, cadence #8c564b -- so a card looks the same every
     time): _draw_profile_metric() plots a ride's per-point array (item [6]'s d["*_pts"],
     aligned with cum_km) as a filled line, and _has_metric_pts() gates a panel per metric,
     so a ride with no HR strap or a plain komoot track simply omits the panel it lacks.
     The figure is a GridSpec(2 + n_prof, 1, height_ratios=[3.0, 1.3] + [0.5]*n_prof) that
     grows only with the metrics present, and the map's bbox is refit to the map axes'
     (narrower) aspect so a tall card doesn't letter-box the near-square basemap.  --profile
     auto-collects the per-point telemetry (with_ext in want_ext, no --ext on the line), and
     when no ride carries any a note is printed and each card falls back to elevation + grade
     only.  Verified by rendering (a 2026-03-02 ride -> map + elev/grade + power/speed/hr/
     cadence panels; a telemetry-free track -> elev/grade only, with the note).

[12] [done] Time-series / trend chart.  A per-ride line chart of distance, gain, duration
     and calories *over time* (all rides on one axis), written to output/ or
     stats/.  Makes training-volume trends and the 2019->2026 span visible.
     DONE (2026-09-21): the `--trend` switch (a standalone figure -- no basemap, no network
     -- so it writes even under --no-map) renders one stacked panel per metric over ride
     date: Distance (km), Elevation gain (m), Duration (h), and a Calories (kcal) panel
     appended only when at least one ride carries a power average.  _trend_panels() places
     each ride on its Wahoo-filename date (_ride_date) and sorts the rides by date, so a
     panel reads left-to-right in time; each panel is a filled line + markers on a shared
     date x-axis (mdates AutoDateLocator + a "%b %Y" formatter), colour-cycled from
     _TREND_COLORS.  A ride with no date in its filename can't be placed, so it is skipped
     and the title notes "N undated ride(s) skipped" (the title also spans first..last date).
     It writes output/trackview-<range>_trend.<format>; --trend auto-collects the telemetry
     (with_ext, always, even under --no-map) that the calories panel needs.  Verified by
     rendering (--trend --no-map -> a 4-panel chart).

[13] [done] Calendar / activity heatmap.  A GitHub-contribution-style grid of the year,
     each day coloured by that ride's distance (or gain / ride count).  A --calendar
     PNG is the best "year at a glance"; reuses _ride_date() per ride.
     DONE (2026-09-22): `--calendar` writes a GitHub-style *activity grid* to
     output/trackview-<range>_calendar.<format> -- one 7 (weekday, Mon-Sun) x ISO-week
     block per calendar year that has rides, stacked top-to-bottom and each labelled with
     its year, every day a square coloured by that day's total on a sequential ramp with a
     pale "no-ride" end, so a busy stretch glows and a rest gap goes blank (a day that saw
     two rides sums them).  The metric is `--calendar-by {distance,gain,rides}` (default
     distance, km/day on a `Greens` ramp; gain = m/day on `OrRd`, rides = a count on
     `Blues`); `--year YYYY` restricts to one calendar year.  All days share one colour
     scale (0 .. the busiest day) so the stacked years compare, with a single colourbar in
     the reserved right margin -- its extent is read off the grid, so it lines up with the
     squares rather than the figure edges.  The x-axis is labelled by month (a tick at the
     first week of each month, not by week number) so a partial year shows only the months it
     spans.  Each ride is placed on its Wahoo-filename date
     (`_ride_date`, the same helper the trend chart uses); an undated ride can't be placed
     and is skipped, the title noting how many.  A standalone figure (no basemap, no
     network), so it writes even under --no-map.  `_calendar_days`/`write_calendar` (with
     `_month_ticks` for the month-labelled x-axis), driven by `_CALENDAR_METRICS` (metric ->
      (label, cmap)) and `_MONTH_ABBR`.  Verified by rendering
     (a single 2026-year grid; a synthetic 2024..2026 stack with per-block year titles and
     the shared colourbar; a no-match --year prints its note and writes nothing).

[14] [done] Annual / monthly summary report.  Aggregate the ride-log per year (and per
     month): total distance, gain, rides, hours, avg power - appended to the stats/
     table or a new per-year report file.  The history spans many years, so a
     year-in-review is currently only possible by hand.
     DONE (2026-09-22): `--summary` writes a per-year, per-month *year-in-review* aggregate
     of the ride-log to stats/trackview-<range>_summary.txt.  `_summary_groups` splits the
     rides by calendar year then month ({year: {month: [rides]}}) plus an 'undated' list
     (no date in the filename, so unplaceable); `write_summary` renders each year as its own
     block of monthly rows (ride count, total distance, gain and hours -- plus, under --ext as
     the ride-log does, the group's point-weighted average power via _metric_avg, a '-' marks
     a group with none) closed by a TOTAL row, and when the log spans several years an
     'ALL years' grand total closes the report, with a note for any undated rides.  The
     columns are sized from every row that prints (so the year blocks align) with the same
     11-space separators as the ride-log.  Pure geometry, so it writes even under --no-map;
     the avg-power column needs --ext.  Verified by rendering (the 2026 log -> a 117-ride
     monthly report with a TOTAL row).

Correctness & data quality
[15] [done] Elevation-gain noise handling.  _distance_and_gain() sums *every* positive
     segment (line 392), so GPS vertical jitter (a few cm) inflates the ascent; add
     a small vertical dead-band or a short elevation smoothing (as for grade, via
     _moving_avg) before gain and the profile fill, behind a flag, so gain_m and any
     climb numbers ([10]) are realistic rather than noise-driven.
     DONE (2026-09-19, fixed same day): --gain-noise M (default 0).  _distance_and_gain
     now sums the rises of a *running climb* - it tracks the climb's top and commits the
     ascent (top - base) only when a descent exceeds M metres, so cm-scale GPS jitter stops
     inflating the figure while real climbs/descents are untouched.  M=0 reproduces the
     classic "sum of every rising segment" exactly (verified: the 115-ride total is 44,533 m,
     matching a hand positive-sum).  The first cut had a 'floor' that never reset on a
     rise, so every point above the running minimum re-added its height above it - over-
     counting ascent by ~800x (a 22,007,339 m total) - and was corrected the same day.
     The wahoo_to_gpx.py copy is left unchanged (its default 0 keeps the two identical).

[16] [done] On-disk tile cache.
     per-detail tiles to disk (keyed by z/x/y) so re-renders and offline runs work,
     and the rate-limit pressure the fallback/backoff in _load_basemap() is already
     trying to absorb disappears.  Pairs naturally with [7]/[8].
     DONE (2026-09-22): contextily already caches the tiles it downloads -- a joblib
     `Memory` keyed by z/x/y per provider -- but it wipes that cache dir at process
     exit (a per-session `tempfile`), so nothing survived a run.  The fix re-points that
     cache at a persistent, git-ignored `tiles/` dir (`TILE_CACHE_DIR`): `main()` calls
     `cx.set_cache_dir(str(TILE_CACHE_DIR))` once, before the render loop (gated on
     `not args.no_cache`; a `--no-map` run skips it, as it draws no tiles), so re-renders
     and offline runs reuse the tiles already on disk instead of refetching every one, and
     the rate-limit pressure the _load_basemap() fallback exists to absorb largely
     disappears.  `--no-cache` (a new map-only flag, ignored under `--no-map`) skips the
     `set_cache_dir` call and leaves contextily's ephemeral per-session cache -- exactly
     the old behaviour.  `add_basemap()` doesn't forward a `use_cache` kwarg, so the only
     lever is the cache *location*; a one-line "tile cache: tiles/ ..." note (suppressed
     by --sparse) confirms it is on.  `tiles/` joined `output/`/`stats/` in .gitignore and
     the flag + cache were documented in the module Usage, README and CLAUDE.md.  Verified
     by rendering: a render populates `tiles/`, and a second identical run with
     `contextily.tile.requests.get` patched to raise (network "disabled") still produced
     the image -- i.e. it was served entirely from the on-disk cache.

Selection, filtering & workflow
[17] [done] Device / sport filters.  The ride-log already groups by Device
     (_device_groups); add --device NAME / --sport NAME (from the GPX <desc>
     "Device:"/"Sport:" lines) to restrict which rides are drawn and listed,
     complementing the --since/--until date filter for mixed-device/sport folders.
     DONE (2026-09-22): `--device NAME` / `--sport NAME` keep only the rides whose GPX `<desc>`
    "Device:"/"Sport:" line equals `NAME`, matched case-insensitively and whitespace-trimmed.
     `_sport_type`/`_SPORT_RE` mirror the existing `_device_type`/`_DEVICE_RE` (both read the line
     `wahoo_to_gpx` stamps into `<desc>`), and `load_tracks` now captures `d["sport"]` alongside
    `d["device"]`.  `_match_meta`/`_filter_by_meta` keep the rides passing *every* active filter
     (a ride missing the line an active filter names is skipped, never guessed, as an undated ride
      is under `--since`/`--until`); when nothing matches, the distinct values the log *does* carry
      are printed per filter (a typo is easy to fix) and the run stops ("nothing to render").  Because
    the values live in the *parsed* `<desc>` (not the filename), the filter runs *after*
     `load_tracks`: `main()` reassigns the `tracks` list, so every downstream reader - the map, the
     ride-log/CSV and the stats/ reports - sees only the kept rides.  They gate the stats too, so they
    are *not* map-only (not in the `--no-map` ignored-flags list; a `--no-map` run still honours them).
     `wahoo_to_gpx.py` needs no change (device/sport parsing lives only here).  Verified by rendering on
    a synthetic mixed folder (2x "Wahoo ELEMNT BOLT"/"Bike" + 1x "Garmin Forerunner 965"/"Run"): the 8
     scenarios - partial match, case-insensitive match, a `--device`+`--sport` AND-combination, and the
    zero-match cases (each printing its found-hints and stopping) - plus a direct check that the filter
     reaches the written ride-log/CSV and the `--split` map render (only the kept rides are written).

[18] [done] --top N.  Render/stat only the N longest, highest-gain, or most-recent rides
     (a "best-of" map, or to keep a huge combined map legible): a --top N --by
     {km,gain,recent} switch, applied after the date filter.
     DONE (2026-09-24): `--top N --by {km,gain,recent}` (default `recent`) keeps only the `N`
      most-significant rides, ranked by `_top_key` -- distance (`km` -> `dist_km`), elevation gain
        (`gain` -> `gain_m`) or the Wahoo-filename ride date (`recent` -> `_ride_date` as a day ordinal,
      a ride with no filename date ranking last).  As with --device/--sport it runs *after* every
      other filter: `main()` reassigns `tracks` once more, right after the --device/--sport block, so
      it selects the top `N` of whatever rides remain and the narrowed list flows to every downstream
      reader (the map, the ride-log/CSV and the stats/ reports).    `_select_top` re-sorts the survivors
      back into the original date-ascending order (the glob is date-sorted) via a stable rank, so the
      ride-log and the `trackview-<first>_<last>` filename read as usual and ties keep that order; `N`
        >= the ride count (or `N` <= 0) is a no-op (the list is returned unchanged, no note).  A one-line
        `--top N --by <by>: K/N ride(s) kept (the N longest/highest-gain/most recent)` confirms a
      narrowing selection -- printed even under --sparse, like the --device/--sport note, whenever the
      set is actually reduced; a no-op selection prints nothing.  Not map-only, so a --no-map run honours
      it; `--by` is an argparse `choices=[km,gain,recent]`, so a bad value is rejected.    `wahoo_to_gpx.py`
      needs no change.  Verified headlessly (--no-map): a unit test of `_select_top`/`_top_key` (each of
      km/gain/recent re-sorted to date order, ties kept, `N`>=count / `N`<=0 a no-op, an undated ride
      last under `recent` yet winning on a pure value under `km`), plus end-to-end runs on the 119-ride
      log -- `--top 5 --by gain` writes the 5 highest-gain rides (confirmed against an independent
      ranking), `--since 2026-08-01 --top 3 --by km` narrows to 34 then the 3 longest of those (the note
      reads 3/34), and `--top 3 --by km --csv` writes a matching 3-row CSV.

[19] [done] Recurse / multiple input dirs.  main() did gpx_dir.glob("*.gpx") (flat, line
     956); nested folders (rglob) are now supported so rides organised under a year/ or
     device/ layout are found.  The repeatable --gpx-dir half is deferred: one recursively-
     searched input dir already covers the real layout (one subfolder per year), and a single
     ride log has no need to pull from several dirs.
     DONE (2026-09-24): discovery now recurses by default -- main() uses gpx_dir.rglob("*.gpx")
     (was glob), so the 787 rides that live one-per-year under gpx/2019..gpx/2026 are all found
     (a flat glob found 0 of them).   `--flat` (a new, *non*-map-only flag, like --since/
     --device/--sport/--top) restores the old single-level glob.  Discovery stays date-ascending
     because the Wahoo filename embeds the ride date (a nested path sorts as gpx/<YYYY>/<date>.gpx),
     so the ride-log order and the trackview-<first>_<last> stats filename read as usual; every
     downstream reader (map, ride-log/CSV, the stats/ reports) sees the nested rides, and
     --since/--until/--device/--sport/--top all compose with it (verified: --since 2026-09-01
     narrows 787 -> 18, --top 3 --by km -> 3).   `_ride_date` reads the date off `path.stem`, so
     a nested path needs no special-casing, and the "no files" message notes "(or its subfolders)"
     when recursing.  The repeatable `--gpx-dir` half of the proposal is intentionally *not*
     implemented.  Verified on the real nested gpx/ (default recurses to 787 rides -> a
     trackview-2019-07-21_2026-09-24 ride-log; --flat finds 0 top-level and stops "nothing to
     render").

[20] [done] --list dry-run.  Print the rides that *would* be processed after all filters
     (names, dates, dist/gain) and stop - no basemap fetch, no image.  A cheap way
     to preview a --since/--until or --top selection.
     DONE (2026-09-24): `--list` is a dry-run preview -- main() calls a new
     `_list_rides(tracks)` and `return`s *before* the basemap fetch, the image and
     every stats/CSV/climbs/summary/trend/calendar write, so a filter selection can
     be previewed cheaply.  It runs *after* all the filters (--since/--until,
     --device/--sport, --top N --by) on the fully-filtered `tracks`, so it lists
     exactly the rides a run would process -- one `Date   Ride   Dist (km)   Gain
     (m)` line each (the ride-log core), closed by a TOTAL row -- and an empty match
     prints "--list: no ride matches the filters".  A ride with no date in its
     filename shows 'undated' (the date filter would already have skipped it, so that
     only arises under --flat or for a foreign name).  It prints its own listing, so
     it overrides --sparse, and it is *not* a map-only flag (a --no-map run stops at
     --list too).  Verified on the real nested gpx/: --list lists all 788 rides,
     --since 2026-09-01 narrows to 19, and --top 10 --by gain shows the 10
     highest-gain rides (re-sorted into date order for display); in every case no
     image or stats/CSV file is written (a `find` of output/ and stats/ turned up
     nothing new).

[21] [done] `--today` -- a shorthand for `--flat --since <today>`.  A one-flag "what did I
     ride today?" preview: it sets the --flat discovery and the --since date bound to
     the calendar date of the run (date.today(), formatted YYYY-MM-DD), so a daily
      "today so far" glance needs no typed date.  Cheap: reuse the existing --flat and
      --since/--until filter (main() would just fold --today into those two args, or
      _parse_argdate it as today), so it composes with --list (a `--list --today` is a
     no-fetch dry-run of today's rides) and the stats path, and is not a map-only flag.
     It is intentionally a *runtime* date (today when you run it), never a baked-in
     constant.
     DONE (2026-09-25): `--today` is the one-flag `--flat --since <today>` shortcut.  main()
     folds it into the two existing knobs *before* discovery: `args.flat = True` (today's ride
     sits at the top level of the folder, not a year/ subfolder) and `args.since =
     date.today().isoformat()` (a *runtime* date, never a baked-in constant), so every
     downstream reader -- the --since/--until date filter, --list and the stats path -- sees the
     narrow selection transparently; a one-line "         --today: flat discovery + since <date>"
     note confirms it.  It is *not* a map-only flag, so `--today --no-map` still writes today's
     ride-log.  Verified on the real gpx/: `--list --today` (flat, not recursive) lists just the
     2026-09-25 ride (1 vs 789 recursive) and writes nothing, and `--today --no-map` skips the
     basemap and writes stats/trackview-2026-09-25_2026-09-25.txt.

Output & sharing
[22] Self-contained HTML / Leaflet export.  A single .html (a tile URL + the tracks
     as inline GeoJSON/SVG) that is zoomable and shareable, complementing the static
     PNG/PDF.  Note the parent repo has a separate interactive gpx_viewer.py
     (Streamlit+Folium); this would be a *static, dependency-light, single-file*
     variant (no server) - a different deliverable.

[23] Contact sheet / gallery.  Tile the --split per-track maps into one mosaic
     image (e.g. a 3x3 of the most recent rides) - a "recent rides" overview without
     opening N files; a --gallery N switch.

Test & testing
The repo has no automated tests; `pytest` 9.1.1 is installed in `.venv` (`.venv/bin/pytest`), so the suite should live in a `tests/` dir run with `.venv/bin/pytest`.  Most of trackview.py is pure geometry / string logic that is cheap to unit-test *without* network or a rendered figure -- only the basemap fetch, the matplotlib image and the stats/report writers are the heavy, I/O-bound paths, so keep those behind fixtures/mocks (or a `slow`/`network` skip marker), not the fast suite.  Numbered after [23].

[24] Scaffold a `tests/` dir + a `conftest.py` with a tiny synthetic GPX fixture (a handful of points -- a climb, a descent, flat -- with and without <extensions> telemetry) built in-memory via gpxpy (no file on disk), plus a fixture that points `--gpx-dir` at a `tmp_path` of two Wahoo-named rides in a subfolder (one carrying a Device:/Sport: desc, one a plain komoot track) so the filter tests compose.  Add a `pytest` line to the README Setup and a `pyproject.toml` `[tool.pytest]` block with a `network`/`slow` marker so CI can run the pure tests fast.

[25] Unit-test the date/parse helpers on their own: `_ride_date` (a Wahoo stem `2026_09_07_08_12_47` -> 2026-09-07; a nested `gpx/2026/...` path; a foreign name like a komoot title -> None) and `_parse_argdate` (a good `YYYY-MM-DD` -> a date; a bad string -> a clear error, no crash).  Pure, no fixtures.

[26] Unit-test the pure geometry: `_grade_cap` (a high-percentile auto cap, a pinned `--grade-cap N`, and the floor/ceil clamp) and `_moving_avg` (a windowed, NaN-skipping average over a known series), and `_select_top` (by `km`/`gain`/`recent`; ties keep date order; `N` >= the ride count or `N` <= 0 is a no-op; an undated ride ranks last under `recent` but can still win on a pure `km`/`gain` value).

[27] Test the filter *composition* against the fixture folder, mirroring the real runs documented above: --since/--until narrows by filename date, --device/--sport by the <desc> line (a non-match prints the distinct values and skips a ride with no line), --top runs *after* them and re-sorts the survivors back into date order, and --today (once [21] lands) folds to `--flat --since date.today()`.  Assert on the surviving `tracks` list, not the printed text.

[28] Lock in the dry-run contract: `_list_rides` prints a header + one row per ride + a TOTAL row, and an empty-set message, and --list writes *nothing* -- so assert via `capsys` that the table appears while a `tmp_path`-based check shows no new file in the stats/ or output/ dirs.  Cover a `--no-map`+`--list` combo and an impossible `--until` (empty match), asserting the pre-existing 'no tracks' guard fires first.
